    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>No primary source gives the numbers. Estimates of the Rus' army run from 8,000 to 80,000 and more, and John Fennell calls many of the figures in the Russian sources formulaic or exaggerated.</li><li>The Rus' princes had no joint commander. Each retinue acted on its own prince's decision, and the host of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> stayed behind a camp on the west bank while the others crossed the river and were beaten.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> did not stay. They pursued the survivors to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, turned east, and came back in 1237 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>On their expedition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> rode 5,500 miles (8,900 km) in three years, and Subutai left spies in Rus' who reported on what was happening there and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

