    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1219</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> invades the Khwarezmian Empire, which breaks up over the next three years</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1221</span><span class="rf-text">A Georgian army is destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khunan">Khunan</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iv-of-georgia">George IV Lasha</a> is mortally wounded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1222</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> persuade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> to leave their alliance with the Alans and then invade the Cuman steppe</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1223</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koten">Köten</a>'s appeal brings the Rus' princes together, and they have the Mongol envoys killed and move down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1223</span><span class="rf-text">On 31 May the allies cross the Kalka River and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and most of the Rus' hosts are routed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1223</span><span class="rf-text">The Kievan camp surrenders after three days and its princes are killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1223</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> turn east and are beaten by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> at Samara Bend</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1237</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and Batu attack again and conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-kalka-river" data-v="3d7e4bae2d" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The allies begin to cross the river</b> The Cuman horse and the Volhynians crossed first, then the Galicians and Chernigovians began to cross. The Kievan host stayed on the west bank and started to build a fortified camp. The Mongol advance guard, main body and right wing waited beyond.</li><li><b>Cumans and Volhynians meet the advance guard</b> The Cumans and the Volhynian host saw the Mongol advance guard and engaged it. The fighting went well for the Rus' at first.</li><li><b>The advance guard falls back and the Rus' lose their order</b> The Mongol advance guard began to retreat and the Rus' chased it. They lost their formation and ran into the main force of the Mongols.</li><li><b>Subutai sends in the main body and the Cumans break</b> Subutai saw that the Rus' princes following the Cumans had fallen well behind and ordered the main body to attack. The Cumans could not withstand the pressure and ran.</li><li><b>The Mongol right wing drives the Cumans into Chernigov</b> The Mongol right wing was quickest to succeed. The Cumans ran to the crossing and crushed and disordered the Chernigov regiments, which were already ready to march.</li><li><b>Mongols attack the Galicians and the Cumans on their flanks</b> With the Cumans and the Chernigov regiments in disorder at the crossing, the Mongols then attacked the Galicians and those Cuman detachments that still stood on their flanks.</li><li><b>Lutsk and Kursk try to help and are beaten</b> Mstislav of Lutsk tried to help first and then Oleg of Kursk, but their hosts were crushed and beaten by the Mongols. Mstislav of Kiev watched the defeat from his camp and made no move to help.</li><li><b>Subutai besieges the Kievan camp and pursues the rest</b> Having beaten the main Rus' and Cuman forces, Subutai set Tsugir and Teshi to besiege the Kievan camp. He went after the survivors with the main part of his army.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1219 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> invaded the Khwarezmian Empire in retaliation for the murder of his ambassadors, and over three years his armies destroyed its forces and the empire broke up. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a>, one of the generals pursuing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Ala ad-Din Muhammad, heard that the Sultan had died, he asked Genghis Khan for a year or two to continue his conquests before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. While awaiting the answer, Jebe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a>, each commanding a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumen</a>, led an army of 20,000 men through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Persian Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, sacking Rey, Zanjan and Qazvin. In late 1221 they entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> again and destroyed a Georgian army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khunan">Khunan</a>.</p><p>Genghis Khan then gave his permission. The crossing of the Caucasus cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a> and hundreds of men lost to the cold. North of the mountains they met an alliance of Lezgians, Alans and Cherkesses, joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, whom the Mongols persuaded to leave it by reminding them of Turkic-Mongol friendship and promising a share of the booty. They routed the rest and then turned on the Cumans, who fled north-west. The Cuman khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koten">Köten</a> went to his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-mstislavich">Mstislav the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and warned him: "Today the Mongols have taken our land and tomorrow they will take yours." The expedition is traditionally regarded as a reconnaissance, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> name the Cumans, the Alans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Rus', <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> included, as its aims.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle.jpg" alt="Primary Chronicle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:14_2_List_of_Radzivill_Chron.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">from the Middle Ages, unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Rus' army came chiefly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, with some from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-mstislavich">Mstislav the Bold</a>, Mstislav III of Kiev and Mstislav of Chernigov were counted the three eldest princes, but the host had no commander-in-chief and each retinue obeyed its own prince. An army sent by Yuri II of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> did not reach the muster. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> were divided into many tribes without a single military organisation; at the Kalka they were commanded by Yarun, the voivode of Mstislav of Galicia.</p><p>No primary source gives numbers, and estimates differ widely. Leo de Hartog puts the Rus' army at 30,000 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at 20,000; Richard Gabriel and Hector Hugh Munro put the Rus' at 80,000, and Gabriel the Mongols at about 23,000. Russian historians of the Soviet era gave 80,000 to 100,000 for the Rus', but recent ones give far lower figures with the Mongols ahead: D. G. Khrustalev (2013) 10,000 Rus' soldiers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and 5,000 Cuman horsemen, Carl Sverdrup 8,000 for the Rus'. Tatishchev gave 103,000 Rus' and 50,000 Cuman horsemen. John Fennell holds that the figures in the Russian sources are formulaic or exaggerated; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle">Novgorod First Chronicle</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> imply 11,000 Rus' soldiers, which he also thinks too many. The Armenian historian Sebastatsi gave the Mongols as 20,000 when they first appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in 1221.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The princes met at Zarub on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. Mongol envoys told them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> had no quarrel with the Rus' and had come against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>; the princes had the envoys killed and the host moved down the Dnieper. Crossing to the left bank, the Rus' met a Mongol advance guard and put it to flight, killing its commander Hamabek. Two weeks later they came to the Kalka and beat a second advance detachment. A council could not decide whether to camp or go forward, and each prince chose his own course.</p><p>On the morning of 31 May the allies began to cross the Kalka. The Cuman horse and the Volhynian retinue went first, then the Galicians and the Chernigovians. The Kievan host stayed on the west bank and began to build a fortified camp. The Cumans and Volhynians met the Mongol advance guard and at first did well; Daniel Romanovich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> was wounded. The advance guard fell back, the Rus' pursued it, lost their order and ran into the main Mongol force. Seeing that the princes behind the Cumans had fallen far back, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> ordered his main body to attack, and the Cumans ran.</p><p>The Mongol right wing, the wing of attack, succeeded fastest. The fleeing Cumans crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> regiments, which were ready to march, and the Mongols then attacked the Galicians and the Cuman detachments still on their flanks. Mstislav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> tried to help, then Oleg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a>, and both hosts were beaten. Mstislav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> watched from his camp and did not move. Subutai left the khans Tsugir and Teshi to besiege the camp and pursued the survivors with the main body.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasilko-konstantinovich"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasilko-konstantinovich.jpg" alt="Vasilko Konstantinovich" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vasilko-konstantinovich">Vasilko Konstantinovich</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%BE_%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87_%D0%B8%D0%B4%D1%91%D1%82_%D0%BA_%D0%9A%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%BA%D0%B5,_1223_%D0%B3.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Richard Gabriel claims the Rus' lost 50,000 men and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> very few. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> gives 10,000 killed, the Nikonian Chronicle, written much later, 60,000, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle">Novgorod First Chronicle</a> gives no figure but says that only one man in ten came home. At least nine princes died, among them Mstislav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, who was lost with his son in the retreat north. The Galician and Volhynian retinues ran to their boats on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and cut up the others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-mstislavich">Mstislav the Bold</a> reached the west bank and destroyed all the boats he could find.</p><p>The Kievan camp had no water. On the third day Ploskinya, a voivode of the brodniks sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a>, swore on the cross that the Kievans would be spared and the princes and commanders freed for ransom. The Mongols, avenging their envoys, broke the promise and attacked as the Kievans came out; some were killed and some taken. The princes and commanders were laid under boards and crushed by the victors, who sat on top to feast. One version holds that the promise had been not to shed blood.</p><p>The Mongol scouts reached Novgorod-Svyatopolch. Hearing that Vladimir troops under the 14-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasilko-konstantinovich">Vasilko Konstantinovich</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> had arrived at Chernigov, they gave up the march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, where the Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> beat them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-samara-bend">Battle of Samara Bend</a>; according to Ibn al-Athir only 4,000 survived. They then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> died soon afterwards.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle.jpg" alt="Novgorod First Chronicle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle">Novgorod First Chronicle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Novgorod_First_Chronicle,_Synod_manuscript_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nikolay Omonov, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No Mongol or Cuman account survives. The Rus' chronicles name the princes who took part and who died but say little of the armies or the casualties. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle">Novgorod First Chronicle</a>, contemporary with the battle and judged the most reliable, blames the flight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>. The Hypatian Chronicle tells in detail only of the centre, where Daniel, Mstislav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> and Oleg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> fought, and links the flight to the arrival of fresh Mongol forces, as does the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> Chronicle for the Cumans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia</a>, writing about 1225, is the only author to give Rus' losses in figures, which he says are very approximate. The Tale of the Battle on the Kalka in the chronicles was probably written soon after.</p><p>The tellings of the fighting differ. In another the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> retreat for nine days, then halt and form up on the Kalka; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> and Kursk open a gap for the fleeing Cumans, and the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> charge through it; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> army, not knowing the battle had begun, collides with the Cumans; smoke disrupts the Rus' command; the Mongol wings close round the Rus', though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-mstislavich">Mstislav the Bold</a> cuts through; and Mstislav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> holds a stockaded camp on a hill by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> with 10,000 men. In that telling a second Mongol embassy declared war; in the one followed here it was let go in peace. The date is also given differently: by one reckoning the fighting ended on 31 May, three days after the main defeat. The Kalka itself is not identified for certain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batu-khan.jpg" alt="Batu Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batu_Khan_on_the_Throne_by_Rashid_al-Din_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid al-Din Hamadani, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> had not come to conquer and went east after plundering. Many principalities lost much of their armies, with Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> the notable exception. The Mongols had crossed most of the Cuman steppe, seen the Rus' forces for themselves and learned from many prisoners how the principalities were organised and fought in different seasons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> left spies in Rus'. More than ten years later the Mongols made their western campaign by the same route in reverse, and in 1237 Subutai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> attacked again and conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>.</p><p>The retinues of the princes and towns were scattered over a huge territory and weakly linked to one another, and gathering a large force was difficult. The battle appears in the chronicles' Tale of the Battle on the Kalka, in novels by Vasily Yan, Isai Kalashnikov and Aleksei Yugov, in a story by Leonid Leonov, and in a painting by Pavel Ryzhenko. A folklore festival at the Kamennye Mogily reserve commemorates those who died.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At the Kalka River on 31 May 1223 a Mongol army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and the Rus' princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, who had no common commander. The Kievan camp then surrendered and its princes were killed. Numbers are only estimates.</em></p>

